======================================================================
13.1 PDA FUNDAMENTALS
======================================================================

1. WHAT IS A PDA?
-----------------

PDA stands for:

    Pushdown Automaton

A PDA is a computational machine that is similar to a finite automaton,
but it has one additional memory structure called a STACK.

Simple idea:

    Finite Automaton
        +
      Stack
        =
       PDA

Why is this important?

A finite automaton has only a finite number of states. Therefore, it
cannot remember an arbitrary amount of information.

A PDA can use its stack as unlimited memory.

This makes a PDA more powerful than a finite automaton.

Most importantly:

    PDA  <-->  Context-Free Languages

A language is context-free if it can be generated by a CFG and
recognized by an appropriate PDA.


----------------------------------------------------------------------
2. WHY DO WE NEED A STACK?
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Consider the language:

    L = { a^n b^n | n >= 0 }

This means:

    ε
    ab
    aabb
    aaabbb
    aaaabbbb
    ...

The number of a's must equal the number of b's.

For example:

    aaabbb

There are:

    3 a's
    3 b's

A finite automaton cannot remember an arbitrary number of a's.

But a PDA can.

It can push one symbol onto the stack for every 'a'.

Then, for every 'b', it removes one symbol.

Example:

Input:

    a a a b b b

Stack:

    push push push
    then
    pop  pop  pop


So the stack acts as memory.


----------------------------------------------------------------------
3. REAL-WORLD ANALOGY
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Think about a stack of plates.

You can:

    PUSH  = put a plate on top

    POP   = remove the top plate

You normally access only the TOP of the stack.

Example:

             TOP
              |
              v
          +-------+
          | Plate |
          +-------+
          | Plate |
          +-------+
          | Plate |
          +-------+
          | Plate |
          +-------+

If you add another plate:

             TOP
              |
              v
          +-------+
          | NEW   |
          +-------+
          | Plate |
          +-------+
          | Plate |
          +-------+
          | Plate |
          +-------+

This is called:

    LIFO

LIFO = Last In, First Out


----------------------------------------------------------------------
4. BASIC PDA ARCHITECTURE
----------------------------------------------------------------------

A PDA has three important parts:

    1. Input
    2. Finite control / states
    3. Stack

ASCII representation:

                     INPUT
                       |
                       v
              +----------------+
              |                |
              |  FINITE        |
              |  CONTROL       |
              |                |
              +----------------+
                       |
                       v
                    STACK


The finite control decides what action should be taken based on:

    • current state
    • current input symbol
    • top stack symbol


----------------------------------------------------------------------
5. MAIN COMPONENTS OF A PDA
----------------------------------------------------------------------

A PDA contains:

    1. States
    2. Input alphabet
    3. Stack alphabet
    4. Transition function
    5. Start state
    6. Initial stack symbol
    7. Accepting states


----------------------------------------------------------------------
6. STATES
----------------------------------------------------------------------

A state represents the current condition of the machine.

For example:

    q0 = reading a's
    q1 = reading b's
    qf = accepting state

ASCII:

    --> q0 --------> q1 --------> qf
       reading       matching
       a's           b's


----------------------------------------------------------------------
7. INPUT
----------------------------------------------------------------------

The input is the string that the PDA must process.

For example:

    input = aaabbb

The PDA reads:

    a
    a
    a
    b
    b
    b


----------------------------------------------------------------------
8. STACK
----------------------------------------------------------------------

The stack is the additional memory of the PDA.

It contains stack symbols.

Example:

    Z0
    A
    A
    A

Here:

    Z0 = bottom-of-stack marker
    A  = memory symbol

Conceptually:

              TOP
               |
               v
             +---+
             | A |
             +---+
             | A |
             +---+
             | A |
             +---+
             | Z0|
             +---+

The PDA normally reads/removes only the top symbol.


----------------------------------------------------------------------
9. STACK ALPHABET
----------------------------------------------------------------------

The stack alphabet is the set of symbols that are allowed inside
the stack.

It is usually represented by:

    Γ

Example:

    Γ = {A, Z0}

This means the stack may contain A and Z0.


----------------------------------------------------------------------
10. INPUT ALPHABET
----------------------------------------------------------------------

The input alphabet is represented by:

    Σ

Example:

    Σ = {a,b}

This means the input can contain only a and b.


----------------------------------------------------------------------
11. INITIAL STACK SYMBOL
----------------------------------------------------------------------

The stack usually starts with a special symbol.

For example:

    Z0

This symbol can indicate the bottom of the stack.

Initial stack:

             +------+
             |  Z0  |
             +------+

After pushing A:

             +------+
             |   A  |  <- TOP
             +------+
             |  Z0  |
             +------+


----------------------------------------------------------------------
12. PUSH OPERATION
----------------------------------------------------------------------

PUSH means putting a symbol onto the stack.

Suppose:

    Stack = Z0

Push A:

    Stack = AZ0

ASCII:

Before:

    +----+
    | Z0 |
    +----+

After PUSH A:

    +----+
    | A  | <- TOP
    +----+
    | Z0 |
    +----+


----------------------------------------------------------------------
13. POP OPERATION
----------------------------------------------------------------------

POP means removing the top stack symbol.

Before:

    +----+
    | A  | <- TOP
    +----+
    | Z0 |
    +----+

After POP:

    +----+
    | Z0 |
    +----+


----------------------------------------------------------------------
14. EPSILON (ε) IN PDA
----------------------------------------------------------------------

ε means:

    "nothing"

or

    "no input symbol is consumed"

A PDA may sometimes make a transition without reading an input
symbol.

Example:

    ε-transition

This is useful when the machine needs to change state or manipulate
the stack without consuming another input symbol.


======================================================================
13.2 FORMAL DEFINITION OF PDA
======================================================================

15. SEVEN-TUPLE DEFINITION
----------------------------------------------------------------------

A PDA is formally represented as:

    M = (Q, Σ, Γ, δ, q0, Z0, F)

Do NOT memorize this blindly.

Understand every component:

    Q   = set of states

    Σ   = input alphabet

    Γ   = stack alphabet

    δ   = transition function

    q0  = initial state

    Z0  = initial stack symbol

    F   = set of final states


Therefore:

    PDA
     |
     +-- Q   -> States
     |
     +-- Σ   -> Input symbols
     |
     +-- Γ   -> Stack symbols
     |
     +-- δ   -> Transition rules
     |
     +-- q0  -> Start state
     |
     +-- Z0  -> Initial stack symbol
     |
     +-- F   -> Final states


----------------------------------------------------------------------
16. TRANSITION FUNCTION
----------------------------------------------------------------------

The transition function is the most important part of a PDA.

A commonly used representation is:

    δ(q, a, X)

where:

    q = current state
    a = current input symbol, or ε
    X = top stack symbol

The PDA may then:

    • change state
    • consume an input symbol
    • replace the top stack symbol

Conceptually:

    δ(current state,
      input symbol,
      stack top)

        ↓

    (new state, new stack content)


----------------------------------------------------------------------
17. EXAMPLE OF A PDA TRANSITION
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Suppose:

    δ(q0, a, Z0) = (q0, AZ0)

Meaning:

    Current state = q0
    Read input = a
    Top stack = Z0

Then:

    Move to q0
    Push A above Z0

Before:

    Input: a...
    State: q0
    Stack:

        A
        Z0

Actually, before the operation:

        Z0

After:

        A
        Z0


----------------------------------------------------------------------
18. ANOTHER TRANSITION
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Suppose:

    δ(q1, b, A) = (q1, ε)

Meaning:

    Read b
    See A at the top
    Remove A from stack
    Stay in q1

Therefore:

    A
    Z0

becomes:

    Z0


======================================================================
INSTANTANEOUS DESCRIPTION (ID)
======================================================================

19. WHAT IS AN INSTANTANEOUS DESCRIPTION?
----------------------------------------------------------------------

An Instantaneous Description, or ID, describes the current situation
of a PDA during computation.

It tells us:

    • current state
    • remaining input
    • current stack contents

A common notation is:

    (q, w, α)

where:

    q     = current state
    w     = remaining input
    α     = stack contents


----------------------------------------------------------------------
20. EXAMPLE OF ID
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Suppose:

    (q0, aabb, Z0)

means:

    Current state = q0
    Remaining input = aabb
    Stack = Z0


After reading the first a and pushing A:

    (q0, abb, AZ0)


After reading another a:

    (q0, bb, AAZ0)


Now the PDA can start matching b's.


======================================================================
PDA COMPUTATION — COMPLETE EXAMPLE
======================================================================

21. PDA FOR a^n b^n
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Language:

    L = { a^n b^n | n >= 0 }

Example strings:

    ε
    ab
    aabb
    aaabbb
    aaaabbbb

Invalid examples:

    aab
    abb
    aaabb
    abab


----------------------------------------------------------------------
22. BASIC IDEA
----------------------------------------------------------------------

The PDA works in two phases.

PHASE 1:
    Read a's and PUSH A for every a.

PHASE 2:
    Read b's and POP A for every b.

Therefore:

    a -> PUSH A

    b -> POP A


ASCII:

                    a / PUSH
                 +-------------+
                 |             v
             --> q0 ---------- q0
                 |
                 |
                 | b / POP
                 v
                 q1
                 |
                 | b / POP
                 |
                 v
                 q1


A more conceptual diagram:

                 a, push A
              +-------------+
              |             |
              v             |
           --> q0 ----------+
              |
              | b, pop A
              v
              q1
              |
              | b, pop A
              +--------+
                       |
                       v
                     qf


The exact transitions depend on the chosen PDA design.


----------------------------------------------------------------------
23. TRACE FOR "aaabbb"
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Input:

    aaabbb

Initial:

    State = q0
    Stack = Z0

--------------------------------------------------
STEP 1
--------------------------------------------------

Read:

    a

Push A.

Stack:

    AZ0

Remaining input:

    aabb


--------------------------------------------------
STEP 2
--------------------------------------------------

Read:

    a

Push A.

Stack:

    AAZ0

Remaining:

    abb


--------------------------------------------------
STEP 3
--------------------------------------------------

Read:

    a

Push A.

Stack:

    AAAZ0

Remaining:

    bb


--------------------------------------------------
STEP 4
--------------------------------------------------

Read:

    b

Pop A.

Stack:

    AAZ0

Remaining:

    b


--------------------------------------------------
STEP 5
--------------------------------------------------

Read:

    b

Pop A.

Stack:

    AZ0

Remaining:

    ε


For three a's there must be three b's, so one more b is processed:

    b -> pop A

Stack:

    Z0


The PDA can now accept.


----------------------------------------------------------------------
24. WHY DOES THIS WORK?
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Suppose the input is:

    a^n b^m

The PDA pushes:

    n copies of A

Then it pops:

    m copies of A

For acceptance:

    n = m

Therefore:

    L = {a^n b^n | n >= 0}


======================================================================
13.3 PDA ACCEPTANCE
======================================================================

25. TWO COMMON ACCEPTANCE METHODS
----------------------------------------------------------------------

A PDA can accept a string using:

    1. Acceptance by final state
    2. Acceptance by empty stack


----------------------------------------------------------------------
26. ACCEPTANCE BY FINAL STATE
----------------------------------------------------------------------

A string is accepted if the PDA finishes its computation in an
accepting/final state.

Conceptually:

    Input
      |
      v
    PDA
      |
      v
    qf

where:

    qf ∈ F


Example:

                 b/pop
              +----------+
              |          v
           -->q0 ------> q1
                         |
                         v
                        qf*

    * = final state


The machine accepts when it reaches qf according to the PDA's
acceptance condition.


----------------------------------------------------------------------
27. ACCEPTANCE BY EMPTY STACK
----------------------------------------------------------------------

A string is accepted when the PDA completely empties its stack
according to the acceptance definition.

Conceptually:

    Input
      |
      v
    PDA
      |
      v
    Stack becomes empty
      |
      v
    ACCEPT


Example:

    Initial stack:

        A
        A
        A
        Z0

After processing:

        Z0

Then the PDA removes Z0 as part of its acceptance procedure.

Stack:

        empty

Therefore:

    ACCEPT


----------------------------------------------------------------------
28. FINAL STATE VS EMPTY STACK
----------------------------------------------------------------------

                 PDA ACCEPTANCE
                       |
             +---------+---------+
             |                   |
             v                   v
       Final State          Empty Stack
             |                   |
       Reach qf              Stack empty
             |                   |
             +---------+---------+
                       |
                  Both can
               characterize CFLs


Important exam point:

    Acceptance by final state
    and
    acceptance by empty stack

are equivalent in expressive power for PDA/CFG language recognition,
although the constructions used may differ.


======================================================================
13.4 PDA CONSTRUCTION
======================================================================

29. GENERAL PDA DESIGN STRATEGY
----------------------------------------------------------------------

When constructing a PDA:

    STEP 1
    Understand the language.

    STEP 2
    Identify what must be remembered.

    STEP 3
    Decide what will be stored in the stack.

    STEP 4
    Decide when to PUSH.

    STEP 5
    Decide when to POP.

    STEP 6
    Define states for different phases.

    STEP 7
    Define transitions.

    STEP 8
    Decide acceptance method.

    STEP 9
    Test valid strings.

    STEP 10
    Test invalid strings.


======================================================================
30. PDA FOR BALANCED PARENTHESES
======================================================================

Consider:

    L = balanced parentheses

Examples:

    ε
    ()
    (())
    ()()
    (()())

Invalid:

    (
    )
    (()
    ())
    (()


----------------------------------------------------------------------
31. BASIC IDEA
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Whenever we see:

    (

push a marker.

Whenever we see:

    )

pop the marker.

Example:

    (()())

Process:

    (   -> PUSH
    (   -> PUSH
    )   -> POP
    (   -> PUSH
    )   -> POP
    )   -> POP

If every closing parenthesis has a matching opening parenthesis,
the stack can return to its initial condition.

ASCII:

              '(' / PUSH
             +----------+
             |          |
             v          |
           --> q0 ------+
             |
             |
             | ')' / POP
             v
            q0


Important condition:

    Never pop when there is no matching opening parenthesis.

At the end:

    no unmatched '('

means the parentheses are balanced.


======================================================================
32. PDA FOR PALINDROMES
======================================================================

A palindrome reads the same forward and backward.

Examples:

    aba
    aaa
    abba
    aabbaa

The difficulty is:

    How do we remember the first half?

The stack can help.

Basic strategy:

    1. Read the first half.
    2. Push symbols onto the stack.
    3. Guess/determine the middle.
    4. Read the second half.
    5. Compare each symbol with the stack top.
    6. Pop when they match.


Example:

    abba

First half:

    ab

Push:

    a
    b

Stack:

    b
    a
    Z0

Then compare:

    b -> pop b
    a -> pop a

Therefore accepted.


For odd-length palindromes such as:

    aba

the middle symbol can be skipped using an ε-transition in a
nondeterministic PDA design.


======================================================================
33. PDA DESIGN TECHNIQUE — PHASES
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Many PDA problems become easier if we divide computation into phases.

Example:

    L = {a^n b^n}

Phase 1:

    Read a's
    PUSH

Phase 2:

    Read b's
    POP

Diagram:

        PHASE 1                 PHASE 2

     +----------+            +----------+
     |          |            |          |
     |  PUSH    |            |   POP    |
     |   A      |            |    A     |
     |          |            |          |
     +----------+            +----------+
           |                       |
           +--------->-------------+
                  switch


======================================================================
34. CFG-GENERATED LANGUAGES AND PDA
======================================================================

A Context-Free Grammar (CFG) generates strings using production
rules.

A PDA can simulate this generation process.

For example:

    S -> aSb
    S -> ε

This CFG generates:

    ε
    ab
    aabb
    aaabbb
    ...

Therefore:

    L = {a^n b^n | n >= 0}

A PDA can recognize the same language.

This gives the fundamental relationship:

    CFG  <---->  PDA

Both describe exactly the class of:

    CONTEXT-FREE LANGUAGES


======================================================================
13.5 CFG ↔ PDA
======================================================================

35. CFG TO PDA
----------------------------------------------------------------------

A CFG contains:

    Variables/non-terminals
    Terminals
    Production rules
    Start symbol

A PDA can simulate derivation.

Suppose:

    S -> aSb
    S -> ε

A PDA can begin with:

    S

on the stack.

Then it can replace S using a production.

For example:

    S
    |
    v

Choose:

    S -> aSb

Stack conceptually becomes:

    a S b

Then process the symbols.

When a terminal is at the top of the stack, the PDA matches it
against the input.

Conceptually:

        CFG
         |
         | production rules
         v
        PDA
         |
         | stack stores symbols
         v
       Input


----------------------------------------------------------------------
36. BASIC CFG → PDA IDEA
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Suppose:

    S -> aSb | ε

Initial stack:

    S

The PDA repeatedly does:

    Variable on stack
           |
           v
    Replace using production

When a terminal is on top:

    terminal on stack
           |
           v
    Compare with input
           |
           v
       MATCH
           |
           v
        POP


For example:

    Input = aabb

The PDA can conceptually derive:

    S
    |
    aSb
    |
    aaSbb
    |
    aabb


Therefore the input is accepted.


======================================================================
37. PDA TO CFG
----------------------------------------------------------------------

The reverse direction is more complicated.

Given a PDA, we can construct a CFG that generates exactly the
language accepted by that PDA.

The detailed construction introduces variables representing suitable
PDA computations between states.

The important conceptual result is:

    PDA language
         =
    CFG language


You do not need to memorize the complicated construction before
understanding the main relationship.


======================================================================
38. EQUIVALENCE THEOREM
----------------------------------------------------------------------

The central theorem of this chapter is:

    A language is context-free
    IF AND ONLY IF
    some PDA accepts it.

In symbols:

    CFL ⇔ PDA-recognizable language


And:

    CFG ⇔ PDA


Therefore:

    Context-Free Language
            |
       +----+----+
       |         |
       v         v
      CFG       PDA


This is one of the most important relationships in Theory of
Computation.


======================================================================
IMPORTANT COMPARISON
======================================================================

39. FINITE AUTOMATON VS PDA
----------------------------------------------------------------------

    +----------------------+----------------------+
    | Finite Automaton     | PDA                  |
    +----------------------+----------------------+
    | Finite states        | Finite states        |
    | No stack             | Has stack           |
    | Limited memory       | Stack memory         |
    | Recognizes regular   | Recognizes           |
    | languages            | context-free        |
    |                      | languages           |
    +----------------------+----------------------+

Example:

    DFA can recognize:

        strings ending in 1

But DFA cannot recognize:

        {a^n b^n | n >= 0}

A PDA can recognize:

        {a^n b^n | n >= 0}


======================================================================
IMPORTANT TERMINOLOGY
======================================================================

40. QUICK DEFINITIONS
----------------------------------------------------------------------

PDA
    Pushdown Automaton; a finite automaton equipped with a stack.

Stack
    A LIFO memory structure used by the PDA.

Push
    Add a symbol to the top of the stack.

Pop
    Remove the top stack symbol.

Input alphabet (Σ)
    Set of symbols that may occur in the input.

Stack alphabet (Γ)
    Set of symbols allowed in the stack.

Initial state (q0)
    State where computation begins.

Initial stack symbol (Z0)
    Symbol initially placed in the stack.

Final state
    A state used for acceptance by final state.

Transition function
    Defines how the PDA changes state and manipulates its stack.

Instantaneous Description
    A representation of the current state, remaining input, and
    stack contents.

Acceptance by final state
    Input is accepted when an appropriate computation reaches a final
    state.

Acceptance by empty stack
    Input is accepted when an appropriate computation empties the
    stack.


======================================================================
IMPORTANT FORMULAS / NOTATION
======================================================================

41. PDA FORMAL REPRESENTATION
----------------------------------------------------------------------

    M = (Q, Σ, Γ, δ, q0, Z0, F)

Remember:

    Q   -> states
    Σ   -> input alphabet
    Γ   -> stack alphabet
    δ   -> transition function
    q0  -> start state
    Z0  -> initial stack symbol
    F   -> final states


A transition is conceptually written as:

    δ(q, a, X) = (p, α)

Meaning:

    Current state       = q
    Input being read    = a
    Stack top            = X

Then:

    New state            = p
    X is replaced by     = α


======================================================================
COMMON MISTAKES
======================================================================

42. MISTAKE 1 — THINKING PDA HAS UNLIMITED STATES
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Wrong:

    PDA has unlimited states.

Correct:

    PDA has a finite number of states.

Its additional memory comes from the stack.


----------------------------------------------------------------------
43. MISTAKE 2 — THINKING STACK IS RANDOM ACCESS MEMORY
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Wrong:

    PDA can access any stack symbol.

Correct:

    PDA normally accesses the TOP of the stack.


----------------------------------------------------------------------
44. MISTAKE 3 — CONFUSING PUSH AND POP
----------------------------------------------------------------------

PUSH:

    add to stack

POP:

    remove top from stack


----------------------------------------------------------------------
45. MISTAKE 4 — THINKING PDA RECOGNIZES ALL LANGUAGES
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Wrong:

    PDA recognizes every possible language.

Correct:

    PDA recognizes context-free languages.


----------------------------------------------------------------------
46. MISTAKE 5 — CONFUSING PDA WITH TM
----------------------------------------------------------------------

PDA:

    stack-based memory

Turing Machine:

    tape-based read/write memory

A TM is computationally more powerful than a PDA.


